.\" Manual page for sessium.
.\" View with:  man docs/sessium.1
.TH SESSIUM 1 "2026-11-14" "sessium 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sessium \- git-like version control for Claude Code and Codex CLI sessions, with Google Drive as the remote
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sessium
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sessium
backs up Claude Code and Codex CLI conversations to Google Drive (via
.BR rclone (1))
and restores them on any machine. Your
.I ~/.claude/projects
tree is the "repo", Drive is the "remote", and a per-machine ledger
(~/.local/state/sessium/state.json) records what was last synced — the
".git" of the arrangement.
.PP
A session id is the transcript file's UUID stem. A prefix of it is accepted
wherever an
.I id
argument appears. A project's
.I munged
name is its absolute path with every "/" replaced by "\-" (leading slash
included), e.g. \fI/home/me/repo\fR → \fI\-home\-me\-repo\fR. Windows munges
every separator and the drive colon the same way.
.PP
Session transcripts are gzip-compressed on the wire, stored on Drive as
.IB <sid> \- <rawmd5> .jsonl.gz
(the md5 of the uncompressed content rides in the name and every download
is verified against it). Legacy plaintext objects stay readable forever.
Everything written locally is plaintext; gzip exists only on the wire.
.PP
File transfers retry twice with a short backoff on timeouts and rclone's
transient errors (exit codes 5, 6, 8), and each transfer's timeout scales
with the transferred size (max 300 s, 10 s/MB) — see ENVIRONMENT for
overrides.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR list " [\-\-titles] [\-\-json] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "]"
List every session found locally or on Drive, newest first. With
.B \-\-titles
shows each session's first real user message. Alias:
.BR log .
.TP
.BR status " [\-\-json] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "]"
Classify every session against the ledger and Drive: in-sync, push, pull,
CONFLICT, new-here, new-there. Read-only; never touches anything.
.TP
.BR check " [\-\-full] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
Six-step self-test: rclone present, remote reachable, folder exists, local
claude dir, ledger, session counts. With
.BR \-\-full ,
additionally audits the ledger — dead entries and Drive orphans — and
spot-verifies a sample of in-sync sessions (local bytes vs the Drive hash).
Read-only; never touches anything.
.TP
.BR setup " [\-\-check\-only] [\-\-encrypted]"
Run the platform setup script (interactive rclone install + Drive OAuth).
.B \-\-encrypted
wraps the Drive folder in an rclone crypt remote, generates a strong
password (printed exactly once) and re-points the config at it.
.TP
.BR push " [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-quiet] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "]"
Upload new and locally-changed sessions. Conflicts are
.B skipped
with a warning — sync or resolve decides them. Never deletes from Drive.
.TP
.BR pull " [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-quiet] [\-\-adopt] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "]"
Download new and remotely-changed sessions. Plain pull skips conflicts like
push. With
.BR \-\-adopt ,
the whole-machine bring-up: every session on Drive not on this machine is
downloaded (conflicts auto-resolved newest-wins — the losing copy is kept
in revert/ and history/, recoverable via
.BR revert ),
then every claude project folder is re-homed to this machine's equivalent
of its repo: the folder name is translated (it is the pushing machine's
munged cwd — its home prefix maps onto this machine's home, so
C--Users-find-Kairo-v1 becomes ~/Kairo-v1); the transcripts' recorded cwd
splits the prefix when it can and is the fallback. Works from any
directory; ~/.claude and ~/.codex are created when missing.
.TP
.BR sync " [\-\-interactive] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-quiet] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "] [\-\-only " \fImemory\fB|\fIskills\fR "]"
Push and pull what changed in one pass, and
.B decide conflicts:
on a terminal it prompts
.BR [L]ocal / [R]emote / [B]oth " "(newest " wins ") / [S]kip ;
without one (cron, pipes) the newest copy wins automatically. The losing
copy is never deleted — it becomes a revert file AND a history snapshot.
.B \-\-only " " \fImemory\fB|\fIskills
two-way syncs the small memory/skills folders without the ledger
(per-file newest-wins, never deletes).
.TP
.BR diff " <id> [\-\-messages] [\-\-json] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "]"
Show one session side by side: local vs remote size, mtime, message count,
last snippet, and the ledger's record. With
.BR \-\-messages ,
show the conversation divergence past the common message prefix.
.TP
.BR resolve " <id> [\-\-interactive] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
Manually decide a conflicted session (same prompt as sync). Non-conflicted
sessions are a no-op.
.TP
.BR revert " <id> [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
List the session's revert copies (kept when conflicts were resolved) and
swap the chosen one back in; the copy it replaces is itself kept. Afterwards
.B sessium sync
pushes the restored copy.
.TP
.BR checkout " <id> <seq> [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
Restore a history snapshot (see
.BR "log <id>" ).
.TP
.BR log " [<id>] [\-\-titles] [\-\-json] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
Without an id: alias for
.BR list .
With an id: list the session's content-addressed history snapshots.
.TP
.BR search " <query> [\-\-semantic] [\-\-json] [\-\-limit N] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "]"
Search sessions: title matches outrank body matches. With
.B \-\-semantic
(and seeker configured) queries a self-hosted seeker engine for semantic
matching instead of literal text.
.TP
.BR seek " [\-\-name " \fIname\fR "]"
Register the local projects tree as a seeker engine source.
.TP
.BR open " <id> [\-\-go] [\-\-adopt] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
Print (or with
.B \-\-go
run) the
.B cd + claude \-\-resume
command for a session. The recorded repo path is used when it exists on
this machine, else the current directory. With
.BR \-\-adopt ,
re-home the session's folder into this machine's name for the CURRENT
directory first (the recorded cwd is the pushing machine's path — adopting
there was a silent no-op). Codex sessions print
.B codex resume <id>
instead (no adoption needed).
.TP
.BR resume " [<id>] [\-\-go] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "] [\-\-project " \fImunged\fR "]"
Pick work up on this machine, whatever machine the sessions came from.
With an id: the session must exist locally (else "run 'sessium pull'
first"); its project folder is adopted into the current directory's munged
name when needed, and the exact resume command is printed.
With no id: local sessions whose recorded repo basename matches the
current directory's basename are listed and their whole folders adopted,
then claude's native picker takes over. Refused when run from the home
directory. Codex needs no adoption — the command is just printed.
.TP
.BR adopt " <munged-folder> <repo-path> [\-\-dry\-run]"
Rehome sessions pulled for a different machine path so
.B claude \-\-resume
finds them here: copies transcripts and data folders (never the machine-local
revert/) under this machine's munged name. Identical files are skipped,
differing ones are kept
.B both
(yours untouched).
.TP
.BR export " [<id>] [\-\-all] [\-\-html] [\-\-out " \fIdir\fR "] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
Write sessions out as Markdown, or with
.B \-\-html
as a static HTML viewer (one page per session plus
.IR index.html ).
Claude transcripts only for now.
.TP
.BR schedule " [\-\-interval " \fImin\fR "] [\-\-print] [\-\-off]"
Manage a scheduled background task running
.B sessium sync
every 30 minutes (systemd user timer on Linux, Task Scheduler on Windows).
.TP
.BR hook " [\-\-print] [\-\-off]"
Manage a Claude Code Stop hook running
.B sessium push \-\-quiet
after every session turn. Only sessium's entries in settings.json are ever
touched.
.TP
.BR gc " [\-\-keep N] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-app " \fIapp\fR "]"
Prune revert/history copies beyond the N newest per session (default 10).
The canonical transcript is never touched.
.TP
.B onboard
First-run report: what's set up (rclone, encryption, Drive, local repo,
ledger, timer, hook) and suggested next steps.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-app " " \fIclaude\fB|\fIcodex\fB|\fIall\fR (default all)
Which CLI's sessions the command touches. Claude and codex trees are never
mixed; the ledger keys are namespaced (codex/<munged>/<id>).
.TP
.B \-\-project " " \fImunged\fR
Restrict a command to one project. Munged names start with "\-", which is
handled correctly in both space and equals forms.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output (list, status, diff, search). Schema in SPEC.md §9.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print exactly what the command would do — push, pull, sync, revert, adopt,
open, resume, checkout, gc — and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-interactive
Force the conflict prompt even when no terminal is attached.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Silence chatter (hooks/cron); errors and conflicts still print.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug " or " \-\-verbose
Print every rclone invocation. Accepted on either side of the subcommand.
.TP
.B \-\-titles
(list) Show session titles.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SESSIUM_CLAUDE_DIR
Claude data root (default ~/.claude).
.TP
.B SESSIUM_CODEX_HOME
Codex CLI data root (default ~/.codex).
.TP
.B SESSIUM_CONFIG
Config file path (default ~/.config/sessium/config.json).
.TP
.B SESSIUM_STATE
Ledger path (default ~/.local/state/sessium/state.json).
.TP
.B SESSIUM_RCLONE_BIN
rclone binary (default "rclone" on PATH).
.TP
.B SESSIUM_SYSTEMD_DIR
User unit directory for `schedule` (default ~/.config/systemd/user).
.TP
.B SESSIUM_COPY_TIMEOUT
Fixed transfer timeout in seconds, overriding the size-scaled default of
max(300 s, 10 s/MB).
.TP
.B SESSIUM_RETRY_DELAY_SCALE
Multiplier on the retry backoff delays (2 s, 4 s); default 1. Set to 0 to
collapse the waits (used by the test suite).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sessium/config.json
{"rclone_remote": "...", "drive_folder": "..."}; legacy ~/sessium/config.sh
(DRIVE_REMOTE / DRIVE_DIR) still read as a fallback.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sessium/state.json
The ledger — per-machine, never synced.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/<munged>/revert/
Machine-local revert copies; never synced.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/<munged>/<id>/history/
Content-addressed snapshots of every overwritten copy; never synced.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success — skips are not failures. 1 on real failures. 130 when
interrupted.
.SH SEE ALSO
rclone(1), claude(1), codex(1). Project docs: docs/MANUAL.md (the user
manual), README.md, SPEC.md, docs/BUILD.md in the repository.
